WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.390
Our library log shows how often each pupil

2
00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:05.590
borrows and how long they keep each book.

3
00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:09.190
Nobody has sorted the pupils into types.

4
00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:11.430
Are there natural groups

5
00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:12.670
hiding in the data?

6
00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.580
Today: how a computer can find groups

7
00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:18.780
in data that nobody has labelled.

8
00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.500
Classification learns from

9
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:23.520
examples that already have labels.

10
00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:26.550
Clustering has no labels at all.

11
00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:28.730
It looks for groups of cases

12
00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:30.330
that sit close together.

13
00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:34.300
First choose k, the number of groups,

14
00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:36.400
and drop k centres anywhere.

15
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:39.660
Each pupil joins the nearest centre.

16
00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:42.730
Then each centre moves to the

17
00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:44.910
middle of its pupils, and we

18
00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:46.670
repeat until nothing moves.

19
00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:50.420
With k equal to three, the library

20
00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:52.320
pupils settled into three groups.

21
00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:56.230
Thirty pupils borrow often, forty borrow

22
00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:59.510
now and then, and twenty almost never.

23
00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.300
Moving a centre is just finding a mean.

24
00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.920
The new centre's position is the

25
00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:08.400
average of its pupils' loans and the

26
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:09.840
average of their days kept.

27
00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:12.630
Careful with names.

28
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.660
The computer only found pupils

29
00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:16.900
with similar numbers.

30
00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:20.770
Calling a group lazy readers is

31
00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:23.610
our story, not the data's, and it

32
00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:25.010
can follow a child around.

33
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:26.700
Quick check!

34
00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.400
A group has three pupils who

35
00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:32.280
borrowed two, four and six books.

36
00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:35.030
Where does the centre move for loans?

37
00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:38.800
To four, the mean of two, four and six.

38
00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.780
The centre always moves to the middle

39
00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:43.180
of the pupils who joined it.

40
00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:46.240
That is the means in k-means.

41
00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:49.750
In the app, open the k-means lab.

42
00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:53.540
Press step and watch pupils change colour

43
00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:56.360
and centres move, then try a different k.

44
00:01:57.680 --> 00:01:58.220
Your turn.

45
00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:00.550
Does clustering need examples

46
00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:02.230
that already have labels?

47
00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:05.620
No, it finds groups on its own.

48
00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:09.090
Remember: choose k, join the nearest

49
00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:12.390
centre, move each centre to its mean,

50
00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:15.770
repeat, and name groups with care.

51
00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:19.540
Try it yourself: plot the sleep and

52
00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:21.620
screen time of a few friends, without

53
00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:23.280
names, and look for groups.

54
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.120
Give each group a kind, neutral name.
